Final Reflection — Shaikha Alalawi

Module: SRM_PCOM7E July 2025 A
Word Count: ~1,000 words

What: My Journey Through Security & Risk Management

Throughout this module, I engaged in a comprehensive learning experience that helped me bridge theoretical concepts of security and risk management with real-world applications. Each unit progressively introduced new layers of understanding, ranging from the fundamentals of threat modelling to advanced disaster recovery planning. One of the initial turning points was learning how to apply the STRIDE model to map out potential threats in a fictional system architecture. This practical activity helped me visualise abstract cybersecurity risks and lay the foundation for structured risk identification. In later units, I contributed to our group project by helping to co-develop the risk register for the Pampered Pets business scenario. My role involved identifying technical and organisational threats, justifying likelihood scores, and aligning each risk with appropriate controls.

As we moved into Unit 6, the importance of regulatory frameworks such as GDPR and ISO 27001 became clear. I authored short wiki entries for each framework in my group and connected them back to our threat models. This allowed us to build traceability between risks, legal obligations, and mitigation strategies. The transition into quantitative risk assessment was both challenging and rewarding. Units 7–9 introduced me to Bow-Tie Analysis (BTA), Fault Tree Analysis (FTA), and Monte Carlo simulations. With tutor guidance and peer feedback, I applied a Monte Carlo approach to evaluate the financial risk of service downtime in our project scenario. I used realistic assumptions and converted qualitative risks into statistical values that informed our decision-making. In Unit 10, we applied these findings to design a disaster recovery architecture that met our target RTO and RPO values, reinforcing the importance of business continuity. Finally, Unit 11 gave us the opportunity to write an executive summary that synthesised all our work into a concise, client-facing document supported by visual evidence and KPI-based validation criteria.

So What: Personal Growth, Emotions, and Lessons Learned

This project-based module challenged me intellectually and personally. In the early weeks, I felt slightly overwhelmed by the number of frameworks and models being introduced. Initially, concepts such as STRIDE and ISO 27005 felt abstract and theoretical. However, working on the group report helped solidify these ideas as we were required to apply them in real scenarios. I was responsible for reviewing different threat modelling techniques and suggesting which ones best suited our use case. This required critical thinking and confidence in presenting arguments to the group, both of which I developed over the course of the project.

Emotions played a surprisingly large role during our team interactions. I experienced moments of stress during deadlines but also felt empowered when my ideas were positively received by peers. One particular moment that stood out was when I successfully demonstrated how a calculated union of probabilities using the Monte Carlo simulation changed our prioritisation of disaster recovery investments. This not only increased my confidence in statistical reasoning but also helped the team align on practical cost-effective solutions. Peer feedback from our mid-project evaluations also helped me recognise that I sometimes took on too much responsibility, especially when trying to ensure work quality. Based on this, I began to delegate tasks more effectively and introduced a shared task board with status updates for transparency.

The biggest turning point for me was shifting from thinking about cybersecurity as simply “adding controls” to viewing it as a dynamic process of managing risk through measurable evidence. Before this module, I viewed risk assessments as a formality. Now, I understand the significance of using metrics like false negative rates, system availability percentages, and vulnerability closure rates to support decision-making. I also came to appreciate the role of recovery tests and acceptance evidence (like tabletop exercises and penetration testing reports) in convincing stakeholders that a DR plan is functional—not just theoretical. Comparing our Unit 6 status document to the Unit 11 executive summary showed me how far our team had progressed in terms of maturity, integration, and traceability.

Now What: Application and Future Professional Use

The knowledge and skills I gained during this module have already started shaping my mindset for future projects, both academically and professionally. First, I will retain the practice of using structured templates to evaluate threats and manage risk throughout the project lifecycle. In the future, I plan to use the STRIDE and BFTA frameworks as default tools when working on IT and security projects, particularly those involving digital platforms or sensitive data. Additionally, I now understand the importance of aligning threat models to business priorities and regulatory obligations, such as GDPR or ISO standards. This ensures that compliance is not a standalone goal, but one embedded into system and team design.

Professionally, I have begun using visual evidence like attack trees and DR flow diagrams to communicate risks to non-technical stakeholders. This has helped me in meetings where technical complexity needs to be translated into business consequences. I’ve also adopted the habit of setting measurable targets (e.g., 95% uptime, 1-minute RTO) and planning tests that validate them, such as simulated failovers. In group settings, I now feel more confident facilitating discussions and introducing tools like shared Kanban boards, regular sprint reviews, and peer retrospectives. These practices have improved both task delegation and personal time management.

Looking forward, I intend to apply these experiences in cybersecurity roles that involve system auditing, disaster recovery planning, or compliance assessments. I am especially interested in risk consultancy work where I can help clients build security solutions that are evidence-based and regulation-aligned. I will also maintain the GitHub repository developed during this module as a digital portfolio showcasing my practical skills, teamwork, and ability to document complex processes in a clear and structured way. This will serve as proof of competency in future interviews or professional certifications.

In conclusion, this e-portfolio reflects not just a series of tasks completed, but a full learning journey where I have grown in knowledge, confidence, and professional maturity. I am proud of the work I have produced, the collaboration I engaged in, and the resilience I developed throughout the SRM_PCOM7E module. By capturing and sharing this journey in a structured repository, I now have a foundation upon which to build further expertise in security and risk management.
